\name{isobaric_kits}
\alias{isobaric_kits}
\title{The isobaric kits mzLib can name, with every channel's label and reporter-ion m/z}
\usage{
isobaric_kits(kit = NULL, timeout = 60)
}
\arguments{
\item{kit}{One kit, matched by mzLib's whole-name rule: case-insensitive, optionally followed by
  \code{" on <motif>"} (\code{"TMT10"}, \code{"TMT6-plex"}, \code{"iTRAQ-4plex on K"}). Never a substring, so
  \code{"TMT10plex"} is refused rather than mistaken for a kit whose name it contains. \code{NULL}, the
  default, lists every kit.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
List the isobaric labelling kits mzLib can name, with every channel's label and reporter-ion m/z.

Calls mzLib's \code{IsobaricMassTag.TryGetIsobaricMassTag} for each \code{IsobaricMassTagType}, or for the
one \code{kit} resolves to through \code{IsobaricMassTag.TryGetTagType}. No file, no network.
}
\details{
\strong{Nothing in the table is typed in.} mzLib holds only the channel labels. Each m/z is a
\code{DI HCD} diagnostic-ion line of the kit's \code{Multiplex Label} modification in mzLib's embedded
\code{TMT.txt}, plus one proton, sorted ascending and paired with the labels by position - the same
m/z MetaMorpheus uses to quantify a TMT search.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_isobaric_kits}: \code{kit}, the name you asked for exactly as given (\code{NA} when
  every kit was listed); \code{kit_count} kits listed; \code{record_count} channels listed over every kit;
  \code{absolute_tolerance}, the half-width in Da of each channel's matching window; \code{kits}, a
  data.frame of \code{kit} and \code{channel_count}, in mzLib's order; \code{caveats}; and \code{records}, a
  data.frame with one row per (kit, channel): \code{kit}, \code{channel_index} (1-based within the kit,
  ascending with the m/z), \code{channel_label} as MetaMorpheus spells it, \code{reporter_ion_mz} (m/z,
  charge 1, theoretical), and \code{mz_min} and \code{mz_max}, the matching window in m/z.

}
\section{What the m/z are not}{


\code{reporter_ion_mz} is theoretical, at charge 1, from mzLib's \code{TMT.txt}: not a calibrated or
observed value. mzLib reads a reporter intensity as the most intense peak between \code{mz_min} and
\code{mz_max}. \code{TMT16} is the lowest sixteen channels of TMTpro 18-plex, and \code{iTRAQ8} has no 120
channel (the phenylalanine immonium ion sits there); its eighth reagent is 121.

}
